Two Pointers Technique (Java / DSA)

The Two Pointers technique is an efficient algorithmic approach where two indices (pointers) are used to traverse a data structure (usually an array or string) to reduce time complexity.

Why Use Two Pointers?

Reduces nested loops

Improves time complexity from O(n²) → O(n)

Common in arrays, strings, linked lists

Types of Two Pointers
1️⃣ Opposite Direction Pointers

One pointer starts from the beginning

Other starts from the end

Move toward each other

📌 Used when array is sorted

Example: Check if a pair sums to target
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int target = 6;

int left = 0;
int right = arr.length - 1;

while (left < right) {
    int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];

    if (sum == target) {
        System.out.println("Pair found");
        break;
    } else if (sum < target) {
        left++;
    } else {
        right--;
    }
}


⏱ Time Complexity: O(n)
📦 Space Complexity: O(1)
